@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.ClassesAndOOP.FieldsAndPropertiesModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Fields and Properties in C#";
}

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <nav aria-label="breadcrumb">
                <ol class="breadcrumb">
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/">Home</a></li>
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item"><a href="/ClassesAndOOP">Classes and OOP</a></li>
                    <li class="breadcrumb-item active">Fields and Properties</li>
                </ol>
            </nav>

            <div class="lesson-content">
                <h1 class="display-4 mb-3">Fields and Properties</h1>
                <div class="d-flex gap-3 mb-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-success">Beginner</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">20 min read</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-primary">Lesson 2 of 11</span>
                </div>

                <p>
                    Fields and properties are how a class stores and exposes its data. They
                    look similar but serve different purposes — understanding the difference
                    is one of the most important OOP fundamentals in C#.
                </p>

                <h2>Fields — raw storage</h2>
                <p>
                    A field is a variable declared directly inside a class. It holds the
                    actual data.
                </p>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    private string firstName;
    private string lastName;
    private int age;
}</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Notice these are <code>private</code> — this is deliberate. Fields are
                    almost always kept private so code outside the class can't set them to
                    invalid values directly (like a negative age). This is called
                    <strong>encapsulation</strong>, covered in depth in Lesson 5.
                </p>

                <h2>Properties — controlled access</h2>
                <p>
                    A property wraps a field (explicitly or implicitly) and exposes it
                    through <code>get</code> and <code>set</code> accessors, letting you add
                    validation or logic around reading/writing the value.
                </p>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    private int age;

    public int Age
    {
        get { return age; }
        set
        {
            if (value < 0 || value > 120)
                throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(value), "Age must be between 0 and 120.");
            age = value;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Now <code>student.Age = -5;</code> throws an exception immediately,
                    instead of silently corrupting your data.
                </p>

                <h2>Auto-implemented properties</h2>
                <p>
                    When you don't need custom logic, C# lets the compiler generate the
                    backing field for you automatically:
                </p>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string LastName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public DateTime EnrolledOn { get; set; }
}</code></pre>
                <p>
                    This is by far the most common style you'll write day-to-day — full
                    manual properties are reserved for cases that genuinely need validation
                    or computed logic.
                </p>

                <h2>Read-only properties</h2>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    public string FirstName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    public string LastName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    // Computed from other properties — no setter at all
    public string FullName => $"{FirstName} {LastName}";
}</code></pre>
                <p>
                    <code>=&gt;</code> here is an <strong>expression-bodied property</strong> —
                    shorthand for a get-only property that computes its value on every access.
                </p>

                <h2>Init-only properties (C# 9+)</h2>
                <p>
                    Sometimes you want a property that can be set when the object is created,
                    but never changed afterward:
                </p>
                <pre><code>public class Student
{
    public int StudentId { get; init; }
    public string Email { get; init; } = string.Empty;
}

// Allowed — set during object initialization:
var student = new Student { StudentId = 1, Email = "ali@example.com" };

// Not allowed — compiler error, Email is init-only:
// student.Email = "new@example.com";</code></pre>

                <h2>Private setters</h2>
                <p>
                    A common pattern: let anyone read a property, but only the class itself
                    can change it.
                </p>
                <pre><code>public class BankAccount
{
    public decimal Balance { get; private set; }

    public void Deposit(decimal amount)
    {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new ArgumentException("Deposit must be positive.");
        Balance += amount;
    }
}</code></pre>
                <p>
                    Outside code can read <code>account.Balance</code> freely, but can only
                    change it by calling <code>Deposit()</code> — which enforces the rules.
                </p>

                <h2>Fields vs Properties — when to use which</h2>
                <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                    <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Use a field when...</th>
                                <th>Use a property when...</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>It's a private implementation detail only the class itself needs</td>
                                <td>The value needs to be exposed outside the class</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>You need a <code>const</code> or <code>readonly</code> value known at compile/construction time</td>
                                <td>You want validation, computed values, or different read/write access levels</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>
                    <strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> almost every piece of public data on a
                    class should be a property, not a public field. Public fields break
                    encapsulation and can't add validation later without a breaking change.
                </p>

                <div class="card border-primary mb-4">
                    <div class="card-body">
                        <h5 class="card-title"><i class="fas fa-pencil-alt text-primary me-2"></i>Try it yourself</h5>
                        <p>Add a <code>GradeLevel</code> property to a <code>Student</code> class that only accepts values 1 through 12, throwing an exception otherwise.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-5 d-flex justify-content-between">
                    <a href="/ClassesAndOOP/01-WhatIsAClass" class="btn btn-outline-primary">
                        <i class="fas fa-arrow-left me-2"></i>Previous Lesson
                    </a>
                    <a href="/ClassesAndOOP/03-MethodsDeepDive" class="btn btn-primary-custom">
                        Next Lesson <i class="fas fa-arrow-right ms-2"></i>
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-lg-3">
            @await Html.PartialAsync("_SidebarNav", Model.SidebarNav)
        </div>
    </div>
</div>